SPF, DKIM, and DMARC passing only confirms that the email was correctly authenticated for the domain outlook-protection.com, not that the domain itself is legitimate or belongs to Microsoft. Attackers often register lookalike domains that resemble trusted services and configure them properly so they pass authentication checks.

In this case, outlook-protection.com is not an official Microsoft domain (such as microsoft.com or outlook.com), so the message is still suspicious despite passing authentication. The email is malicious because it uses a trusted brand (“Microsoft 365 Security”) to trick the CFO into reacting to fake security alerts, which is a common credential phishing technique.

Authentication verifies domain handling, not brand authenticity or intent, so a properly authenticated email can still be a phishing attempt if the sending domain is deceptive.
